{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L6YYnWtDbyWj2USEVojpMD","type":"tool_result","content":"== B01 gap Minimal tool surface\n viol [\"Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called\"] also ['B05']\n sum The extractor sub-agent silently gets the full procurement tool list, and ERP operations are allowed by prefix, not by name.\n RF app/tools/__init__.py:69 Extractor role name mismatch falls back to the procurement tool list.\n NO B01.1 app/tools/__init__.py:69 | Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash | extractor.py:25 uses role \"line_item_extractor\" but config/tools.yaml:14 defines \"extractor\"; the lookup falls back to the full procurement list, so t\n NO B01.2 app/gate.py:35 | Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them. | Discovered ERP MCP operations reach the model through erp_call, filtered only by prefix (policy.yaml:12-16 \"vendor.\", \"invoice.\"), which admits vendor\n NO B01.3 app/mcp_client.py:117 | The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically. | erp_call dispatches any operation the MCP server lists by name; the only allowlist is the prefix test in gate.py:35. Unknown top-level tools are rejec\n== B02 gap Least-privilege access\n viol ['Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries'] also []\n sum Tenant scope in memory_read is chosen by the model, and write scopes are mintable through the generic erp_call tool.\n RF app/tools/memory.py:44 Model-controlled business_unit in memory_read crosses the tenant boundary.\n RF config/policy.yaml:23 operation_scopes grants write scopes (erp.vendor.write, erp.invoice.write) to operations reachable through the generic erp_call tool.\n NO B02.2 app/tools/memory.py:44 | Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant a | memory_read takes the tenant from a model-supplied argument (schema allows business_unit), so the model can read another unit notes. ERP and documents\n== B04 gap Independent revocation\n viol ['Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect'] also []\n sum No revocation time limit is stated, and cached tokens are used until they expire.\n NO B04.3 app/identity.py:97 | Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is s | No revocation limit is stated anywhere in the repo. Tokens are cached until expiry and no introspection, deny list or CAEP is used.\n== B05 gap Destructive-action interception\n viol [\"When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed\"] also ['B01']\n sum The gate lets erp_call write operations and non-flagged Slack messages through unapproved, and approvals do not bind arguments.\n RF app/gate.py:35 erp_call prefix check admits write operations (vendor.update_remit_to, invoice.mark_paid, invoice.void) with no approval.\n RF app/gate.py:40 External-send approval depends on a model-set boolean, not on the channel.\n RF app/approvals.py:77 Approval is bound to tool and target only; amount, payee and lines are not compared.\n NO B05.1 app/gate.py:47 | High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive upd | policy.yaml:5-7 lists only create_purchase_order and pay_invoice. Unlisted tools default to allow. vendor.update_remit_to, invoice.mark_paid and invoi\n NO B05.2 app/approvals.py:77 | The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments | find_valid matches tool, target (invoice_id or source_doc_id) and tenant only; args_sha256 is stored but not compared, so amount, payee_account_id, ve\n NO B05.3 app/gate.py:35 | The same gate covers every