rnal false posts to #vendor channels without approval.\n RF app/approvals.py:77 Approval is matched on tool and target only; the stored args digest is not compared, so amounts or payee can differ after approval.\n NO R02.2 app/loop.py:101 | Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request. | Only read_document output carries a source label; ERP results, memory notes and extractor output reach the model as unlabeled redacted JSON, and the d\n NO R02.3 app/gate.py:35 | Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could caus | Prefix 'vendor.' and 'invoice.' admit vendor.update_remit_to, invoice.mark_paid and invoice.void through erp_call with no approval; post_slack_message\n NO R02.4 prompts/system.md:24 | Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector. | Remit-to changes are stopped by prompt wording and the ingest detector, with no code block behind them (gate.py allows vendor.update_remit_to).\n NO R02.5 tests/test_gate.py:81 | Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads | The only injection test runs once with one plain-text payload and asserts pass/fail; no repeats, adaptive or schema-valid variants, or success rates e\n== R05 gap Memory isolation and write validation\n viol ['Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved', 'Check what gets written to memory'] also []\n sum memory_read takes the business unit from a model argument, and write validation is a newline-bypassable regex list.\n RF app/tools/memory.py:44 memory_read scopes by a model-supplied business_unit, allowing cross-tenant note reads\n NO R05.1 app/tools/memory.py:44 | Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access | memory_read takes business_unit from the model-supplied argument, and the tool description invites cross-unit lookups. No check ties it to the caller'\n NO R05.2 app/tools/memory.py:21 | Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored | The only validation is regexes where . does not match newlines. 'Ignore\\nprevious instructions' passes all four patterns and is stored, then served ba\n== R11 gap Tested kill switch\n viol ['It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks'] also []\n sum The halt flag is read only when a run starts, so running work is not stopped.\n RF app/loop.py:127 Stop is checked only at run start; in-flight runs, tool calls and tasks are not stopped\n NO R11.1 app/loop.py:127 | A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set. | Halt is read once at run start. The loop (loop.py:139-174) and extractor (extractor.py:64-97) never re-check before model or tool calls, so a run in p\n NO R11.3 app/loop.py:83 | Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set. | No stop handler exists. Running tool calls are not cancelled on halt; they end only on their own HTTP timeouts (payments.py:28 timeout=30.0).\n NO R11.4 app/main.py:159 | The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks. | Run tasks are detached and untracked, so nothing can cancel them on a halt. Only the sweeper is cancelled, at app shutdown (main.py:113).\n== R13 gap Full execution audit\n viol ['Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions'] also []\n sum Run-level failures and budget stops inside a tool call leave no linked audit step.\n RF app/loop.py:150 Run-level failures (model-call errors, halt at start) and budget stops inside a tool are not recorded as linked steps\n NO R13.1 app/loop.py:150 | Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and | The run try block handles only BudgetExceeded (loop.py:175). A model-call or other run-level exception, and the AgentHalted raised at loop.py:127 befo\n NO R13.4 app/loop.py:85 | Tool errors, timeouts, and retry attempts are recorded as linked execution steps, so failed actions can be reb | A BudgetExceeded raised inside tool.invoke is re-raised with no tool_error step. The run-level budget_exceeded record (loop.py:176) has parent=run_nod\n== A01 gap Distinct agent identity\n viol ['Preserve any explicitly delegated user/system subject separately and bound the authority used'] also []\n sum The token broker caches STS tokens by tenant, so a token carrying one user's delegation is reused for other users.\n RF app/identity.py:95 TokenBroker cache keyed by audience, scope and tenant only; delegated_subject is not in the key, so one user's delegated token serves other users.\n NO A01.2 app/identity.py:95 | Tool and API clients use short-lived credentials with a verified acting-agent identity and any separately boun | Token cache key omits the delegated subject and run. A token minted with one user's actor_token is reused (identity.py:97-98) for any other user in th\n NO A01.3 app/identity.py:97 | For user-initiated runs, the original user is a verified delegated subject while the acting agent retains its  | Cached token minted for user A is returned for user B in the same tenant, so the verified original subject is not bound per run. Also actor_token at i\n== A02 gap All six identity fields\n viol ['successful, denied, failed, and background actions'] also []\n sum Refused (halted or over-quota) runs, approval decisions and extractor failures leave no audit record.\n RF app/loop.py:127 Halt/quota refusals happen before any audit record; no record for the refused run.\n RF app/main.py:183 Approval decisions via Slack callback are applied with no audit row (approval_decided never recorded).\n NO A02.2 app/loop.py:127 | All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful ma | A halted run raises AgentHalted before run_started (loop.py:133) and no audit row is written; same for quota refusal at loop.py:129. Slack approval de\n== A03 gap Content-derived type identity\n viol ['Compute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt'] also []\n sum The type ID hashes prompt file paths, not prompt contents, so prompt edits keep the same ID.\n RF app/identity.py:68 System prompt enters agent_type_id as a path string only; content changes do not alter the ID.\n RF app/identity.py:69 Extractor prompt enters agent_type_id as a path string only.\n NO A03.1 app/identity.py:68 | The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model  | The hash takes the prompt file path, not its sha256 or content, so editing the system prompt does not change the type ID.\n NO A03.2 app/identity.py:69 | The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skippe | Extractor prompt is also hashed by path only; prompt files defining behavior are not covered by the hash.\n== A04 gap Trustworthy attribution\n viol [\"Don't accept labels the model supplies\"] also []\n sum memory_read lets the model name any business unit and reads that tenant's notes.\n RF app/tools/memory.py:44 memory_read accepts model-supplied business_unit and queries that tenant's notes; tenant isolation depends on the model.\n NO A04.1 app/tools/memory.py:44 | Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produc | memory_read uses the model-supplied business_unit (schema line 112 exposes it) to choose whose notes to read instead of the verified tenant.\n NO A04.2 app/tools/memory.py:56 | Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the v | The supplied tenant label is passed straight into the SQL query and logged in tool_proposed args, not rejected or overwritten; erp tools do ov